uch smaller country and we have far a  few  fewer TV stations than he would have  here do the anchors go out and make  speeches for money they can do yeah I  mean you have to get permission and all  of that from your bosses do you vote  when you're in the Irish media as a  private individual yes you do yeah yeah  and you have you did you vote all the  time yourself well so I would but since  I've lived in the u.s. we had a general  election and you can't vote from abroad  so I wasn't able to cast a vote for the  current dole dole air in the current  Parliament that's sitting there and in  that general election I was out of the  country and that's that where did you  grow up in Ireland I grew up in Dublin  in the capital city and just on the  south side and foothills of the Dublin  Mountains one of the kind of suburbs  just on the edge of the city there what  kind of a families you come from your  parents your other kids in the family  yeah so I'm the eldest of three girls  and both parents worked and working the  media or anything like that so I'm the  first one to really break through into  that sphere so through a lot of hard  graft as you know yourself and nothing  comes easy and in this life but  particularly if you want to get into  journalism in Ireland  you've got to work very hard and do all  your apprenticeships and all of that  kind of thing so I always wanted to be a  journalist since as far back as I can  remember I was writing poetry and novels  and did little mock radio show in my  bedroom with all that kind of stuff and  then went off to university and did my  undergraduate in journalism and then  after I guess seven or eight years after  graduation went back while working  full-time to a master's in international  relations part-time as well because I've  always been fascinated by world affairs  and geopolitics and all of that which is  why I wanted to come to Washington as a  foreign correspondent because you know  if you're into international affairs and  how the world works  this is really the center point of that  where did you go to school on Ireland I  went to school in a secondary school  called sanctuary College which was a  sisters  see all girls school and in Ireland  again it's different to hear the you  know most people go to public schools  and it is changing a little but mostly  they would be same sex schools  traditionally run by various religious  organizations so mine was just at the  end of my road and but despite that I  was late a lot even though my journey  about three minutes  when did the journalism kick in like I  mean as long as far back as I can  remember really like said I was I was a  small kid always writing stories and all  of that and just really fascinated with  how the world worked and if if something  was the rule or the regulation but why  is it like that and who makes those  rules and regulations and and what  happens in other countries you know  Ireland very small island nation with an  immigrant history so we're very outward  looking rather than inward looking we're  always looking to what's going on  elsewhere and maybe relations in America  or Australia or Europe or whatever so I  just a ferocious reader as well and as a  child and a teenager and just really  loved just broadcast journalism in  particular because of the immediacy of  us now a nice growing up there wasn't as  immediate as it is now a 24-hour news  cycle and everything's on your phone you  know and that was very much the era of  the one broadcast today or whatever but  and you know I just love that you get to  see so much happen in the world like we  get to have front seat to history as the  saying goes and that's an incredible  privilege to have as your job where did  you meet your husband  and he's a he's a he was a friend of a  friend but he's an incredibly private  person who doesn't work in the media and  doesn't like me talking about him in the  media so you have to keep some things  personal in this 